{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChFPZayjGJbVtw8kQM7oP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote five findings to `.redeye/findings.md`, most severe first. I found them by reading the code. None is caught by a test, and the build and full suite still pass (200 of 200).\n\n1. **A resumed loop never runs the install command.** When a ticket resumes, `runTicketAttempt` (`src/orchestrator.ts:2406`) recreates the worktree and goes straight into the loop. The fresh path runs `runInstall` first, and `runTicketFixStep` only mentions `installCmd` in the prompt without running it. In an npm project, the fixer's tests then run with no `node_modules` and fail, so a resumed ticket with open findings fails every time. The resume tests don't set `installCmd`.\n2. **Committing each log entry can collide with other commits, which blocks the resume it was added for.** `recordEntry` calls `commitProgress` in the main checkout while other tickets in the wave are also committing. Nothing makes those commits wait for each other, so two can collide on `.git/index.lock`. A failed commit is only logged. If the run is interrupted before the end-of-wave commit, the next run stops with \"Working tree is dirty\" and can't resume. Preventing that is exactly why `AGENTS.md` says entries are committed one at a time.\n3. **With a command-line provider override, the review log names the wrong implement role.** `src/index.ts:888` always passes the config's `implementStr`, so `redeye claude -m opus` over a `pi -m qwen` config records `pi -m qwen` in every round. The spec says each round records the role strings actually used.\n4. **Resume can skip a failed test gate.** When the fixer's tests fail, the round entry (with `testResult:'fail'`) and the `failed` terminal entry are committed separately. If the run is interrupted between the two, `detectResume` sees a matching head and no terminal entry. It then resumes without a test gate, and a clean review merges a branch whose tests fail.\n5. **The reviewer gets `--prefer-subagent` even on providers that don't support it.** `src/index.ts` warns about and drops it only for the implement provider. A `pi` reviewer still gets the subagent rule in its prompt, which breaks the documented rule that unsupported options are warned about and dropped. The same may apply to `--max-turns`, but I didn't check how `pi` handles that one.\n\nI didn't change, stage or commit anything."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":184,"cache_read_input_tokens":96201,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":184},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eb7d7135-9979-477c-b8fa-32eb07614eca","uuid":"84199fa2-66f1-4f39-89ba-2944e9183586","timestamp":"2026-11-21T03:48:39.386Z","request_id":"req_011ChFPZaesqGmrpJ9qSK7xW"}
